<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=world-wide-web">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">World Wide Web</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as WWW</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The World Wide Web is a global system of linked documents and other resources shared over the Internet, invented at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cern">CERN</a> in 1989 by the English computer scientist Tim Berners-Lee. He submitted his proposal in March 1989 and had a working system by the end of 1990, with a browser called WorldWideWeb and an HTTP server; the Web was released to the whole Internet on 23 August 1991. After CERN made its code available royalty-free in April 1993 and the Mosaic browser appeared, thousands of websites sprang up in less than a year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/world-wide-web.jpg" alt="World Wide Web" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Coolcaesar at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Web_Server.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
